## ES6 Classes vs ES5 Constructor Functions

### ES5 Approach
```js
function Animal(name) {
  this.name = name;
}
Animal.prototype.speak = function () {
  return `${this.name} makes a noise.`;
};

function Dog(name) {
  Animal.call(this, name);
}
Dog.prototype = Object.create(Animal.prototype);
Dog.prototype.constructor = Dog;
Dog.prototype.bark = function () {
  return `${this.name} barks.`;
};
```

### ES6 Class Approach
```js
class Animal {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }
  speak() {
    return `${this.name} makes a noise.`;
  }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  bark() {
    return `${this.name} barks.`;
  }
}
```

### Key Differences
| Feature | ES5 | ES6 Class |
|---|---|---|
| Syntax | Verbose | Concise |
| Inheritance | Manual prototype wiring | `extends` / `super` |
| Methods enumerable | Yes | No |
| Call without `new` | Returns `undefined` or global pollution | Throws `TypeError` |
| Hoisting | Function declarations hoisted | TDZ applies |

### Important: Still Prototype-Based
Classes are **syntactic sugar** — the prototype chain is identical to the ES5 equivalent.

